WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:06.033 --> 00:00:09.600
Few people in the history of art have managed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:13.920
to capture moonlight the way&nbsp;
John Atkinson Grimshaw did.

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:19.520
In his paintings it is almost always night,&nbsp;
and it has almost always just rained. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:23.760
moon peeks out from behind the clouds,&nbsp;
and its silvery light reflects off the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:27.840
wet cobblestones, the puddles,&nbsp;
and the waters of the harbors.

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:31.280
Along the sides of the streets,&nbsp;
the bare branches of the trees&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:35.360
stand out against the night sky.&nbsp;
And in the middle of the darkness,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:41.120
the yellow glow of a streetlamp or a window&nbsp;
reminds us that someone is still awake.

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:45.760
But the most impressive thing about his works&nbsp;
is the precision with which everything is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:51.760
painted. Every branch, every reflection, and&nbsp;
every glimmer of light seems so real that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:57.040
in his time, some critics even doubted&nbsp;
whether they were truly paintings.

00:00:57.040 --> 00:01:04.240
John Atkinson Grimshaw was born in 1836 in Leeds,&nbsp;
an industrial city in the north of England.

00:01:04.240 --> 00:01:06.880
And if there is one thing&nbsp;
that defines his childhood,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:12.960
it is that no one in his family wanted him to&nbsp;
be an artist. His parents were strict Baptists,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:19.120
and his father was a policeman. For them, painting&nbsp;
was not a profession, but a waste of time.

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:23.280
They pushed him toward a secure job,&nbsp;
and at the age of sixteen Grimshaw&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:26.960
began working as a clerk for the&nbsp;
Great Northern Railway company.

00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:32.240
But in his spare time, he painted. And&nbsp;
his family's reaction was so hostile that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:36.240
as the story goes, his mother once&nbsp;
threw his paints into the fire,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:40.240
and even cut off the gas supply to&nbsp;
the room where he painted at night.

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:47.440
Even so, Grimshaw did not give up. And in 1861,&nbsp;
at the age of twenty-four, he made the decision&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:53.840
that horrified his parents, leaving his job at the&nbsp;
railway to devote himself entirely to painting.

00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:59.120
In his early years, he was influenced by the&nbsp;
Pre-Raphaelites, and especially by another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:06.000
painter from Leeds, John William Inchbold. He also&nbsp;
followed the ideas of the critic John Ruskin, who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:13.360
championed "truth to nature," the idea of painting&nbsp;
the world exactly as it is, in absolute detail.

00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:20.720
So during the 1860s, Grimshaw painted extremely&nbsp;
precise still lifes and landscapes. And he did so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:26.000
well that, by the end of that decade, he could&nbsp;
already afford to rent a seventeenth-century&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:31.760
mansion on the outskirts of Leeds, which&nbsp;
would later appear in many of his paintings.

00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:36.960
But it was in the mid-1870s that he found&nbsp;
the subject that would make him immortal.

00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:41.760
Night is the most recurring theme in his&nbsp;
paintings. He called them his "moonlights."

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:46.000
In them we often see the streets of&nbsp;
Leeds, the docks of Liverpool and Glasgow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:51.040
the River Thames in London, or the harbor&nbsp;
of Whitby. All of them painted at night,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:54.080
under the light of the moon or of gas streetlamps.

00:02:54.080 --> 00:02:58.720
In Grimshaw's world, the season&nbsp;
is nearly always autumn or winter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:03.040
night is always falling, and a&nbsp;
rain shower has only just ended.

00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:08.160
And it is precisely this that made his work&nbsp;
stand out among the other artists of his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:12.960
time. Although the tradition of painting&nbsp;
landscapes by moonlight already existed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:18.480
it was almost always done in rural scenes.&nbsp;
Grimshaw, however, brought that tradition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:25.280
into the Victorian city. Painting the factories,&nbsp;
harbors, and wet streets of industrial England.

00:03:25.280 --> 00:03:30.960
And he did it with an almost photographic&nbsp;
realism. His images are sharp, precise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:37.600
and detailed. But at the same time, they convey&nbsp;
a deeply romantic and melancholic feeling.

00:03:37.600 --> 00:03:43.600
Now, how did a painter with no academic&nbsp;
training achieve such a level of realism?

00:03:43.600 --> 00:03:46.960
The answer is as fascinating&nbsp;
as it is controversial.

00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:50.960
But before we talk about this, I would&nbsp;
like to take a few seconds to remind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.960 --> 00:03:55.040
you that if you would like to have one of&nbsp;
Grimshaw's paintings in your collection,&nbsp;&nbsp;

