00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:29.760
As I was saying, Grimshaw never fully&nbsp;
mastered drawing or perspective. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:32.160
instead of fighting against that limitation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:38.080
he found another way to solve it. He used a&nbsp;
camera obscura or photographs as the basis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.080 --> 00:04:43.920
for his compositions. And on occasion, he&nbsp;
even painted directly onto the photographs.

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:48.320
He also had another trick. He mixed sand&nbsp;
into his paint to give his light effects&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.320 --> 00:04:54.080
extra sparkle. That is why the reflections on&nbsp;
his puddles and cobblestones seem to glimmer.

00:04:54.080 --> 00:04:59.680
For many of his contemporaries, this was almost&nbsp;
cheating. Some critics said his works showed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.680 --> 00:05:05.200
no marks of brushwork, and others even doubted&nbsp;
whether they could be considered paintings at all.

00:05:05.200 --> 00:05:07.920
But there was someone who did not think that way.

00:05:07.920 --> 00:05:08.080
El Reconocimiento De Whistler

00:05:08.080 --> 00:05:12.400
James McNeill Whistler was one of&nbsp;
the most famous painters of his time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:16.160
and also one of the most vain.&nbsp;
He was known precisely for his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.160 --> 00:05:21.200
"Nocturnes," night scenes painted&nbsp;
in a hazy, almost abstract style.

00:05:21.200 --> 00:05:26.160
And when he saw Grimshaw's work, he said&nbsp;
a phrase that is still remembered today:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.160 --> 00:05:32.160
"I considered myself the inventor of nocturnes&nbsp;
until I saw Grimmy's moonlit pictures."

00:05:32.160 --> 00:05:36.320
Coming from a man like Whistler, it&nbsp;
was an enormous compliment. And the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.320 --> 00:05:40.800
most curious thing is that their styles&nbsp;
were completely opposite. While Whistler&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.800 --> 00:05:44.960
dissolved the night into patches of&nbsp;
color, Grimshaw painted it with such&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.960 --> 00:05:49.840
sharpness that people wondered whether they&nbsp;
were looking at a painting or a photograph.

00:05:49.840 --> 00:05:55.520
During the 1870s, Grimshaw was at the height&nbsp;
of his career. In addition to his mansion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.520 --> 00:06:00.640
he rented a second house in Scarborough,&nbsp;
which he named "Castle by the Sea."

00:06:00.640 --> 00:06:05.280
But in 1879 he suffered a&nbsp;
serious financial disaster,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.280 --> 00:06:10.720
the exact causes of which are still unclear,&nbsp;
and he had to leave the house in Scarborough.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.720 --> 00:06:15.360
From then on, bailiffs coming to collect&nbsp;
debts became part of his family's life.

00:06:15.360 --> 00:06:19.920
And his personal life was also&nbsp;
marked by tragedy. His wife gave&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.920 --> 00:06:23.840
birth to around fifteen children,&nbsp;
but only five or six survived.

00:06:23.840 --> 00:06:29.520
To support his family, Grimshaw painted&nbsp;
six days a week. Whistler even mocked his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.520 --> 00:06:34.640
productivity. But for him, it was&nbsp;
not an obsession, but a necessity.

00:06:34.640 --> 00:06:41.360
Until finally, Grimshaw died of cancer in&nbsp;
1893, at his mansion on the outskirts of Leeds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.360 --> 00:06:45.360
at the age of fifty-seven and in rather&nbsp;
difficult financial circumstances.

00:06:45.360 --> 00:06:46.080
Cierre

00:06:46.080 --> 00:06:50.480
Today, the man whose mother threw his&nbsp;
paints into the fire is considered one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.480 --> 00:06:54.800
of the great painters of the Victorian&nbsp;
era. And his moonlights remain some of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.800 --> 00:06:58.800
the most evocative images of&nbsp;
the entire nineteenth century.

00:06:58.800 --> 00:07:01.920
And although he may have used&nbsp;
photographs in his paintings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.920 --> 00:07:07.840
or never learned to draw like the great masters,&nbsp;
in the end he achieved something no one else did:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.840 --> 00:07:11.600
painting the night exactly as&nbsp;
it feels to walk through it.

00:07:11.600 --> 00:07:17.280
I would love to know what you think. Does using&nbsp;
photographs take away from the merit of his work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.280 --> 00:07:22.000
or is the final result the only thing&nbsp;
that matters? Let me know in the comments.

00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:27.680
As always, thank you for watching the video until&nbsp;
the end, and I will see you in the next video.

